# Exact-E4 bounded attribution and one-executor hybrid brief

21 October 2026. This is an execution brief for a Medium implementor, not a claim that E4 has achieved parity. It refines the dense proposal in `fresh-architecture-speed-zoomout.md`: the current packed `AlignedField` lanes can be updated in place, but `RetainedRope::update_ordered_ranges` is deliberately transactional and rebuilds changed leaves. Do **not** replace that failure-atomic rope policy with unchecked mutation merely to call a path “in place.” Product work remains held until this discriminator identifies a plausible owner.

## Freeze and one capture

1. Confirm clean `HEAD=16bd60e747a1323536e02f547841325ca96c43ff`; record `git status --porcelain`, HEAD/tree, exact hashes of `packages/glyph/src`, `packages/glyph/rust/shaper/src`, `packages/glyph/package.json`, relevant package build scripts and lockfile. The E4 archived package is `/Volumes/Depot/Developer/glyph/.cache/held-gather-input-plan-20261020/evidence/candidate/pmndrs-glyph-0.1.0.tgz`, SHA-256 `9b1ad97a03f5af8eaa0613e5bdaab601edf7fccf69fdeacc6059759191b1fb18`; its named Wasm is the sibling `shaper-profile/text-shaper.named.wasm`, SHA-256 `806066af37452bbf5781955c8bb6f2083997cc68fd0e5eae485612ea5b608826`, with shipping/executable section SHA-256 `d14d1fc68071da0127addec4dcb044998a7da4b16b32fa9c3a84eb7c70cc1018`. The named manifest truthfully records `b81756a5` plus a dirty `codec_gather.rs`; that is **not** by itself an exact-HEAD proof. `git diff --stat 399cc792..HEAD` for package source/scripts/package manifest was empty and the E4 `codec_gather.rs` hash matched HEAD (`e8c21cb9...`), but authenticate the **whole** package product input tree and archive member set, not just that one file. A preliminary 666-input freeze check found four current-versus-E4 differences: two Labs harness helpers, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, and `pnpm-workspace.yaml`. Record their exact paths/hashes/diffs and classify each as harness, dependency resolution, or package build input. Product-source identity plus authenticated frozen archive bytes can justify profiling that **archived E4 product**, but cannot make its historical build environment identical to current HEAD. The current peer/tool resolution must also be pinned in the new profile manifest, and cross-era sample differences remain descriptive. Use archived `consolidated-source-freeze.json` and `candidate/package-dist-comparison.json` only after checking their own receipts and the current files. Hash archive bytes and all shipping members, especially `dist/three/text.js`, `dist/internal/render-planner.js` and `dist/text-shaper.wasm`, before and after the run. If the old archive cannot be proved to represent current E4 product, or the lock/workspace differences touch its package build output in a way that cannot be bounded, run the named package `build` and `pnpm pack --pack-destination <new ignored evidence dir>` once from clean HEAD, then `glyph:shaper-profile-artifact --artifact <new tgz> --source-root <checkout> --output <new ignored evidence dir>`. Its executable-section equality check must pass. Do not use the current `dist` merely because it exists; it may contain E6.
2. Under the pinned `/Users/mini/.local/bin/mise exec -- pnpm scripts run` driver, run **one** `benchmark:edit-sized-publication-profile` capture against the authenticated exact-E4 archive with `--case bulk-write --boundary publication --count 1024 --position first --warmups 10 --iterations 80 --reads none --publication-path scene --wasm <authenticated E4 named Wasm> --artifact <archive> --output <fresh ignored evidence dir>`. This is 1,024 **distinct** short Bitmap labels per callback and one scene publication, with no timed query; the workflow alternates `alpha`/`bravo` values and invokes `created.scene.updateMatrixWorld(true)`. Do not pass `--phase-observer old-new-1024`: that mode accepts only three older archive hashes and disallows `--wasm`. Save `cpu-profile.json`, `summary.json`, `manifest.json`, stdout/stderr, tool/fixture/source hashes and exit code. A profile is a locator of cost, not a seven-family acceptance result.
3. Inspect this profile's call tree, then write `ancestry.json` with **new, disjoint** `{label,nodeId}` anchors at `updateBulk`'s label-set subtree and the sibling `updateMatrixWorld` publication subtree. Never reuse E2's node 7/50. Find the profile's exact named-Wasm URL and run `benchmark:cpu-profile-summary --profile .../cpu-profile.json --output .../exclusive.md --manifest .../manifest.json --artifact <same archive> --wasm-url <exact URL> --ancestry .../ancestry.json`. Save the ancestry file, summary, raw profile and the small script/fixture hashes so a reviewer can rederive every grouping. If V8 inlines the relevant setter into `updateBulk`, name and retain the larger enclosing node and mark that share unresolved; do not force a fabricated split.

The primary command, assuming the reuse proof passes, is:

```sh
/Users/mini/.local/bin/mise exec -- pnpm scripts run benchmark:edit-sized-publication-profile -- \
  --artifact /Volumes/Depot/Developer/glyph/.cache/held-gather-input-plan-20261020/evidence/candidate/pmndrs-glyph-0.1.0.tgz \
  --wasm /Volumes/Depot/Developer/glyph/.cache/held-gather-input-plan-20261020/evidence/candidate/shaper-profile/text-shaper.named.wasm \
  --output /Volumes/Depot/Developer/glyph/.cache/held-architecture-zoomout-20261021/evidence/e4-1024-named-profile \
  --case bulk-write --boundary publication --count 1024 --position first \
  --warmups 10 --iterations 80 --reads none --publication-path scene
```

Run it from the checkout only after the archive/member/tool/fixture checks; do not treat the command's success as those checks. The optional preparation-only continuation uses the same command with a fresh output directory and `--boundary preparation`; it is conditional on an ambiguous first profile. `benchmark:cpu-profile-summary` then consumes the first capture with its exact observed Wasm URL and newly selected ancestry IDs.

For this first capture use the existing native E2 Bitmap ledger as a *work-shape reference* only: 1,024 warm query preparations, 1,024 HarfRust calls/10,240 units, zero publication shaping, 10,240 publication source rows, 9,216 selected rows, 109,568 f32 reads, 97,280 f32 writes, 589,824 compared and 368,640 emitted physical bytes. The replay includes query-result bytes that the E4 no-read timing lane omits, and it is neither an E4 counter nor elapsed cost. The E7 actual-Bitmap boundary receipt rules out one short-window shortcut for that corpus; it does not price shaping.

## Attribute only what the capture can identify

Record exclusive sampled time and share **inside each disjoint phase**, with total samples and unresolved/GC counts. Keep JS sourcemap frames and named Rust frames separately. Setter producer chain: `render-planner.ts::_updateText`, `#prepareTextCandidate`; `handle-state.ts::prepareParagraph`; transport request writer / Wasm `measureParagraph`; `state.rs::measure_paragraph_inner`, `validate_planner_inputs`, `validate_lifecycle_input`, `prepare_semantic_inputs(Single)`, staged text/Unicode/bidi/shape/cluster/flow/position and adoption. Classify `HarfRust`, allocation helpers and unresolved Wasm only when the actual named stack supports it. Source already proves the empty-lifecycle fast return, single-owner no-sort and inactive Unicode swap; do not label those as repeated scans or a duplicate Unicode pass without a captured counterexample.

Publication consumer chain: `render-planner.ts::#compileFrame` / `Glyph.shape` or Three `updateMatrixWorld`; `state.rs::prepare_update_inner`, `prepare_retained_order`, retained root dispatch; `codec_gather.rs::append_retained_stream`, `update_retained_source`, `update_fields`, `gather_fields`; `retained_rope.rs::update_ordered_ranges`; `ordered_plan.rs` compare/write and Codec executor; renderer host-buffer/Three upload. Separately retain “other JS,” “other Wasm,” and GC. The stock stage classifier groups `shaping_state`/`harfrust`, `codec_gather`/`append_planner_gather`, `ordered_plan`/compiler, transport/wire and allocator names but does **not** comprehensively classify `state.rs` preflight or un-named/inlined work. Therefore inspect actual named frames and source ancestry rather than equating stage totals with those operations. Do not convert sampled self time into a per-call wall coefficient, add setter/publication p50s, or estimate GC/cache/branch costs from V8 samples. The old main/published/E2 phase medians are historical profiled controls, not a matched E4 marginal ablation.

The profile's built-in post-timing bulk assertions check all authored text, positive measurement and committed glyph availability; they do **not** construct cold independent labels for bulk-write or prove physical byte/patch equality. Add a **separate untimed** cold oracle before acting on a patch: create each final short label independently under the same authenticated package/fixture, publish it, and compare `measure`, glyph IDs/placements and accepted state against its warm peer; compare the packed plan bytes, patch ranges, batch order and draw topology where the existing product test/oracle exposes them. Reuse named package tests/fixtures; if no existing named oracle covers physical comparison, record that gap and require it in the implementation gate rather than silently upgrading the profile's assertions. No timed read should be inserted into the profiled loop.

## Decision after the capture

Use the **observed removable owner**, not an arbitrary percentage threshold:

- If named setter samples concentrate in repeated transport/admission/adoption code *outside* required shape/flow and the specific repeated operation is visible in source, write a one-owner setter-fusion design that deletes that operation while retaining synchronous errors, inactive candidate preparation, per-set adoption and immediately prepared queries. A no-op setter or same-label overwrite is only a negative control: it cannot stand in for 1,024 distinct final semantic states. Do not claim that a single request can elide those 1,024 states under the accepted contract.
- If samples and source show significant root retained-gather/gap/owner-range work in the **publication** subtree, while setter time is mostly irreducible semantic work, prototype the dense same-source-count path described below. Compare its preflight plus traversal against the current `append_retained_stream` path; deletion of general gap/prefix/owner apparatus must outweigh the new preflight on the dense lane. The E2 work ledger can suggest what to count, but only exact-E4/new-candidate counters or direct source counts can support a removed-work claim. The current profile by itself cannot prove a dense speedup.
- If both groups are small compared with the complete-operation deficit, or samples are dominated by shape/flow, GC, unresolved Wasm or renderer host work, do not start either patch. The minimum additional observation is one **matched preparation-only** `bulk-write/1024/reads none` capture with the same archive/named Wasm, source freeze and warmup/iteration counts. That removes publication from the callback and checks whether setter ancestry persists; it still does not isolate request crossing from semantic preparation. If that also leaves the owner unresolved, report the exact indistinguishable frames and request a narrowly scoped, test-only attribution change at that boundary. No new ABI, speculative cache or broad matrix is justified by ambiguous samples.

There is no claim that five buckets (request, transaction, semantic, gather, renderer/GC) can be priced exactly from one V8 profile. The concrete stop test is whether the maximum *plausibly removable* sampled subtree is large enough to motivate one bounded patch relative to the observed dense gap; a subtree smaller than the gap is only a rejection signal if its classification and sampling uncertainty make that upper bound credible. Final proof remains unprofiled complete-operation parity against both controls, not a sample share.

## Dense path implementation sketch, only if chosen

Use **one** prepared semantic owner representation, **one** `CodecGatherWorkspace` (`sources`, selected glyphs/placements/masks and aligned f32/u32 lanes), and the existing Codec/ordered-plan executor. Place admission in `state.rs::prepare_update_inner`, after current dirty-owner, order and checkpoint information exists, before `append_retained_stream`. Require: an accepted retained root; no checkpoint/lifecycle/order/decoration topology change; every active owner dirty; new positioned source count equals its old owner range; and a preflight in source order proving selected-versus-recordless count, one-to-one old/new record ordinal, selection/program/batch compatibility and safe changed-mask/stable-ID correspondence. A changed identity with `ALL_SEMANTIC_CHANGES` may be admitted only if the current retained update's topology rule already accepts it. Reject to the existing sparse/replacement/full path **before** modifying any lanes if proof fails. No ratio threshold substitutes for these invariants.

Once admitted, form one contiguous source range and traverse it with the existing `RetainedRope::update_ordered_ranges` transaction. Have a dense-specific **adapter**, not a second Codec implementation, feed the same `update_retained_source`/`update_fields` input policy without `OwnerRanges` clean-gap jumps or a `previous_sources` snapshot solely for prefix summaries. The rope still copies changed leaves/branches transactionally; the packed lanes and selected `PlanGlyph` slots already support indexed overwrite. Reserve all fallible capacity and validate bindings/programs before the first lane mutation, or stage only the minimal rollback state required by existing owner semantics. If the existing helper cannot uphold rollback with no second full packed snapshot, reject this candidate rather than weaken accepted-generation/in-flight guarantees. Keep Codec scalar/SIMD program, wire format, batch ordering and ordered-plan comparison unchanged. Work is O(N + G + GF) for N owners/G sources/F declared fields, O(1) per-record new scratch, with current transactional rope-copy cost; it cannot remove required full changed output or 1,024 setter shape calls.

Deletion targets are the dense use of `append_retained_stream`'s cloned `previous_sources` gap-prefix machinery, repeated owner-range admission/cursor resets and any now-dead dense full-reset branch. Preserve that machinery for sparse gaps and count/topology changes. Do **not** keep E5/E6 locator arrays or E3's clear-and-repopulate fallback in parallel. Feasibility proof is source-local: `GatherSource` stores stable ID/selection; `AlignedField` and `PlanGlyph` support indexed updates; `update_retained_source` already performs selection/topology checks and calls `update_fields`; `update_ordered_ranges` already provides one transactional ordered walk. The hard unresolved detail is whether the existing helper's mid-walk lane writes are rollback-safe when a later callback fails. The implementor must establish this with code/test evidence **before** applying the path; if not, use a bounded staged update or stop. Count preflight, source rows, rope leaves copied, field reads/writes, allocations and physical output; a reduction in f32 reads alone is insufficient.

After one candidate, run cold independent semantic/physical/rejection-retry oracles, then the fixed seven-family single-call matrix from a newly frozen shipping archive against same-machine main and published 0.1.0, E2 continuity, complete p50/p95/tails, exact exclusions and drift. Preserve size, ABI, peer, engine and whole-app memory gates. No stack merge/release/tag/demo merge follows until parity is established; up to three coherent post-parity boost attempts remain a separate decision.
